<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="680" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">How the appraisal clause works</span></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>How the Insurance Appraisal Clause Works in Idaho</h1><p class="lede">Seven stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what and where claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-w" href="/schedule-a-consult/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>7</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><p class="toc-t">The seven stages</p><ol><li><a href="#step-1">Pin down the disagreement</a></li><li><a href="#step-2">Send a written demand</a></li><li><a href="#step-3">Each side names an appraiser</a></li><li><a href="#step-4">The appraisers select an umpire</a></li><li><a href="#step-5">Inspection and line-by-line work</a></li><li><a href="#step-6">Unresolved items go to the umpire</a></li><li><a href="#step-7">Two signatures make the award</a></li></ol></nav>
<p>Appraisal is a dispute-resolution process written into most property insurance policies. It is narrower and usually faster than a lawsuit, and it focuses on one question: how much the covered loss is worth. This guide walks through the seven stages as they typically unfold for Idaho property owners. Your policy’s exact wording controls, so read it alongside this guide.</p>

<h2 id="step-1">1. Pin down the disagreement</h2>
<p>Appraisal fits when the insurer has accepted that a loss is covered, at least in part, and the two sides disagree about the cost of repair or replacement. Typical examples: how many roof slopes need replacing after hail, whether smoke-damaged cabinets can be cleaned, or what local contractors charge to rebuild a snow-crushed garage.</p>
<p>If the insurer has denied coverage, or the dispute turns on an exclusion, a late-notice defense or the meaning of a policy term, appraisal is usually the wrong tool. That’s a legal question for an Idaho attorney.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Before you demand</b>Make sure you have the insurer’s full itemized estimate, your own itemized estimate and good photos. Appraisal works best when both sides’ numbers are on paper.</div>

<h2 id="step-2">2. Send a written demand</h2>
<p>Most appraisal clauses say that either party may demand appraisal in writing if the two sides fail to agree on the amount of loss. The demand should identify the claim and policy, state that you disagree on the amount of loss, invoke the appraisal provision and, ideally, name your appraiser. Send it in a way you can prove, and keep a copy.</p>
<p>Some policies require that certain steps be completed first, such as submitting a proof of loss. Check for those conditions before sending the demand.</p>

<h2 id="step-3">3. Each side names an appraiser</h2>
<p>Each party chooses its own appraiser, and many policies require that person to be competent and impartial or disinterested. A common deadline is 20 days after the demand, but wording varies. Your appraiser should have no financial stake in the repair contract. Disclose any relationships early to avoid later challenges to the award.</p>

<h2 id="step-4">4. The appraisers select an umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on a neutral umpire. If they can’t agree within the time the policy allows, many clauses let either party ask a court in the county where the property is located to appoint one. The umpire’s fee is usually split between the insured and the insurer.</p>

<h2 id="step-5">5. Inspection and line-by-line work</h2>
<p>The appraisers inspect the property, exchange estimates and work through the differences. Most disputes narrow sharply at this stage. Measurements get reconciled, missing items are added or removed and pricing is aligned. Many panels finish here with two appraiser signatures and no umpire involvement at all.</p>

<h2 id="step-6">6. Unresolved items go to the umpire</h2>
<p>Items the appraisers can’t agree on go to the umpire. Depending on the panel, that may involve written submissions, a site visit or an informal hearing. The umpire decides each disputed item based on the evidence presented.</p>

<h2 id="step-7">7. Two signatures make the award</h2>
<p>An award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss. A well-drafted award separates replacement cost and actual cash value and may break out categories such as dwelling, other structures and contents. After the award, the insurer applies the policy’s terms, including the deductible, prior payments and any holdback, and issues payment.</p>

<h2>Timeline and cost</h2>
<p>A straightforward residential appraisal often takes two to three months from the demand to the award. Complex commercial or contents-heavy claims take longer. Each side pays its own appraiser and usually half of the umpire’s fee. Ask for any fee arrangement in writing before work starts.</p>

<h2>Idaho context</h2>
<p>The Idaho Department of Insurance regulates insurers doing business in the state and accepts consumer complaints about claim handling. Idaho Code Title 41 includes rules on unfair claim settlement practices, and Idaho courts recognize claims against insurers for bad-faith handling of first-party claims. Idaho law also provides for attorney fees in some suits where an insurer fails to pay the amount justly due within a set time after proof of loss. Those are legal matters outside the appraisal process; if they may apply, talk to an Idaho attorney.</p>
